# ── Sign-in ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# There is no credential in this file. FabOrchestrator is the only identity:
# every visitor signs in with their own FO account, which FO verifies against
# its own user table, and this app holds only the session that results.
#
# A local DEMO_USER_* pair used to be accepted. It was removed on 2026-09-01
# because it minted a session with no FabOrchestrator token — one that reached
# the app, opened an agent, and could not ask it anything. Set
# FABORCH_BASE_URL below; that is what makes sign-in work.

# ── Signing keys for this app's own session token (plan RP2 part 5) ─────────
#
# Every token names the key that signed it, so a key can be rotated without
# signing anybody out. REQUIRED, both of them: the app refuses to sign or check
# a session without a key id, or with a secret shorter than 32 characters.
#
#   SESSION_SIGNING_KEY_ID   a short PUBLIC label for the current key, e.g. 2026-09.
#                            Not a secret: it is written into every token. Never
#                            derive it from the secret.
#   SESSION_SIGNING_SECRET   the current secret, at least 32 characters:
#
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
#
#   SESSION_SIGNING_PREVIOUS_KEYS   optional, verify-only: id:secret pairs,
#                            comma-separated (so a secret must not contain a comma).
#
# Secrets are used exactly as set: they must not begin or end with spaces.
#
# Rotation, with nobody signed out:
#   1. SESSION_SIGNING_PREVIOUS_KEYS=2026-09:<the old secret>
#   2. SESSION_SIGNING_KEY_ID=2026-10 and a new SESSION_SIGNING_SECRET
#   3. after one SESSION_TTL_HOURS (12 h), remove the previous pair.
# Removing a key early signs out whoever still holds a token it signed.
SESSION_SIGNING_KEY_ID=2026-09
SESSION_SIGNING_SECRET=replace-with-at-least-32-random-characters
# SESSION_SIGNING_PREVIOUS_KEYS=

# How long a signed-in session lasts, in hours.
SESSION_TTL_HOURS=12

# How long the FabOrchestrator cookie outlives the session, in seconds
# (plan RP2: default 300, allowed 1-900). Within it, a phone that comes back
# after its session expired still gets FabOrchestrator's session revoked.
# SESSION_COOKIE_GRACE_S=300

# This app's public origin, e.g. https://faborch-demo.fly.dev. Optional for
# now: sign-in and sign-out refuse a request whose Sec-Fetch-Site header says it
# came from another site, which every supported browser sends; with this set, an
# Origin header that names another site is refused too (lib/same-origin.ts). Set
# it per app: an app deployed under another name from the same config would get
# a wrong value.
# PUBLIC_ORIGIN=


# ── The connection to FabOrchestrator ─────────────────────────────────────────
#
# This app computes nothing about manufacturing. It forwards a question to the
# running FabOrchestrator, and FO's agents and their approved tools answer it.
# Point this at the FabOrchestrator you want answers from.
#
# Locally that is `claudeai_athena` (`npm run dev` in a clone of the
# FabOrchestrator product repo, which serves :3000 and needs its own
# DATABASE_URL and ANTHROPIC_API_KEY — those belong to FO, not to this app).
#
# **Required.** Since the demo credential was removed this is the only way to
# sign in at all: unset, the login route answers 503 and says so, rather than
# telling a visitor their password is wrong.
#
# There is no default on purpose. A localhost fallback would mean a deployment
# that forgot this setting silently answered from whatever happened to be on
# :3000, and the failure would look like strange answers rather than a missing
# setting.
#
# ── MUST be https, except on localhost ────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Sign-in sends the operator's FabOrchestrator PASSWORD over this connection,
# and every call after it carries their session token; the questions and the
# plant data come back the same way. Over plain http all of it is readable by
# anything on the path.
#
# `foBaseUrl()` enforces this and there is no override:
#
#   https://anything                 accepted
#   http://localhost:3000            accepted — a FabOrchestrator on this machine
#   http://127.0.0.1:3000            accepted
#   http://anything-else             REFUSED, with a message saying why
#
# The exception is keyed on the host, not on NODE_ENV — `npm start` runs an
# ordinary local build with NODE_ENV=production, and a loopback address is
# unreachable from elsewhere by construction, which is the stronger guarantee.
# Note `http://localhost.example.com` is a REMOTE host and is refused.
#
# Production (fly.toml) points at https://d7y8a8whrch88.cloudfront.net.
# CloudFront terminates TLS; the Elastic Beanstalk origin behind it also answers
# on plain http, and pointing at that directly is exactly what this guard stops.
#
FABORCH_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Optional. Which model FabOrchestrator should answer with.
#
# Defaults to `claude-opus-4-8` — FO's own default, which it presents as
# "FabOrchestrator 2.0" (`GET /api/chat`). FO validates the id against its
# `model_registry` table and against the signed-in user's role, so an id that
# deployment does not allow comes back as an error rather than a silent
# substitution. Only set this if a deployment allows a different set.
#
# FABORCH_MODEL=claude-sonnet-5


# ── The embedding gateway ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# How much of FabOrchestrator this origin serves, through the gateway
# (`app/fo-gateway/[...path]/route.ts`, `lib/gateway/registry.ts`):
#
#   FO_EMBED_MODE=whole     FabOrchestrator's own pages and API, the target
#                           (plan RP1). This app keeps only /login, /offline,
#                           /diagnostics and the front door.
#   FO_EMBED_MODE=surfaces  only the pages listed in FO_EMBED_SURFACES; the
#                           rollback step.
#   unset / off             no FabOrchestrator here: this app's own screens.
#
# The API is always an explicit allow-list, in every mode.
#
# FO_EMBED_MODE=whole
# FO_EMBED_SURFACES=/chat,/reports
#
# FabOrchestrator's screens come from FABORCH_BASE_URL, like its API: the real
# FabOrchestrator. A separate UI build (a preview of an unshipped FO change) can
# answer the pages and assets only when BOTH of these are set; FO_UI_BASE_URL
# alone makes every FabOrchestrator page fail closed (plan RP1, G18):
#
# FO_UI_SPLIT_ALLOWED=1
# FO_UI_BASE_URL=http://faborch-fo-ui-preview.internal:3000
#
# Request bodies: at most 20 MiB (attachments included), refused above that
# with a coded 413. Next hands the app up to 25 MiB (next.config.ts) so an
# oversized body is measured rather than arriving cut short. Not configurable
# here; see lib/gateway/body-limit.ts.


# ── For the check scripts only ────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# A FabOrchestrator account that `scripts/*.mjs` signs in as when running the
# regression, security and journey checks against a deployment. **The app never
# reads these** — it has no service account and no shared FO identity, because a
# demo is not a reason to hand whoever holds the URL somebody else's MES access.
#
# See docs/OPEN_ISSUES.md §1: the shared demo password reached the Fly request
# logs on 2026-09-03 and is due for rotation.
#
FABORCH_PROBE_EMAIL=
FABORCH_PROBE_PASSWORD=


# ── Time limits on this app's own calls to FabOrchestrator (optional) ─────────
#
# `lib/faborch/client.ts` gives every call it makes a limit, named after the
# plan's RP4 call classes. The defaults are provisional (CP3 sets the real
# values) and must stay under the edge's own origin timeout. The gateway's
# forwarding has its own limits, below.
#
# FO_TIMEOUT_BOUNDED_MS=15000        sign-in, /me, lookups: headers and body
# FO_TIMEOUT_STREAM_START_MS=60000   how long an answer may take to begin
# FO_TIMEOUT_REVOKE_MS=5000          FabOrchestrator sign-out
#
# Gateway deadlines (lib/gateway/deadline.ts, plan RP4). Per class, three
# limits: HEADERS (until FO's response headers), IDLE (FO silent after
# them) and LIFE (the whole body). Provisional until CP3; must stay under the
# edge's origin timeout, and the stream IDLE above FO's 20 s keep-alive.
#
# GATEWAY_TIMEOUT_BOUNDED_HEADERS_MS=15000   _IDLE_MS=15000   _LIFE_MS=120000
# GATEWAY_TIMEOUT_STREAM_HEADERS_MS=60000    _IDLE_MS=45000   _LIFE_MS=360000
# GATEWAY_TIMEOUT_UPLOAD_HEADERS_MS=180000   _IDLE_MS=30000   _LIFE_MS=300000
# GATEWAY_TIMEOUT_DOCUMENT_HEADERS_MS=20000  _IDLE_MS=20000   _LIFE_MS=120000
#
# OWNERSHIP_TTL_MS=1800000           how long a proved conversation owner is
#                                    remembered (the plan's T_own, RP6)
#
# KEEP_READING_MAX_STREAMS=50        a stopgap (lib/gateway/keep-reading.ts):
#                                    how many chat answers the gateway may keep
#                                    reading after the phone has gone, so that
#                                    FabOrchestrator saves them. 0 turns it off.
#
# LOG_STACKS=1 adds error messages and stacks to the JSON error lines
# (`lib/report-error.ts`). Development only: messages can carry addresses.


# ── Which device started which conversation (required) ───────────────────────
#
# Several people may sign in with the same FabOrchestrator account; each device
# sees and uses only the conversations it started (`lib/gateway/seats.ts`). The
# record of who started what is one append-only file. It must be on storage
# that survives restarts: losing it hides every conversation inside this app
# (nothing is lost in FabOrchestrator). With the variable unset every
# conversation route answers 503; there is no in-memory fallback.
#
# Locally, any writable path:
SEAT_STORE_PATH=./.seat-store/conversations.log


# ── What is deliberately NOT here ─────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY — this app makes no model call. Every answer comes from
#   FabOrchestrator, which holds its own key. A key here would break the
#   boundary the whole design rests on, and `scripts/security-review.mjs`
#   fails if one appears on the deployment.
#
# DATABASE_URL — this app has no database. The production-order workflow and
#   its decision log were removed on 2026-09-01; the session is a stateless
#   signed token bound to the FabOrchestrator cookie it was minted beside.
